  "prompt": "Pricing now wants a longer, steadier baseline: the 28 days just before each promotion's first day, that first day not included, holding every sales line of the promotion's products in those days, whatever promotion the line carries. Because the periods differ in length, compare them per day. For each promotion that started in 2025 (start_date from 1 January to 31 December 2025), give the units per day on promotion (over the promotion's own days, its first and last day both counted), the units per day in the baseline (over 28 days), and uplift_per_day, the first minus the second, each rounded to 2 decimals with ROUND. Work out uplift_per_day from the unrounded values. A sales line is on a promotion when it carries that promotion's ID in order_lines.promo_id, whatever its date; where two promotions overlap on a product, a line carries only the deeper one, so it counts once. Days are sales.order_date, the UTC day of the order. A period with no sale counts 0 units. Return four columns, in this order: promo_id, promo_units_per_day, baseline_units_per_day and uplift_per_day. Return one row for each such promotion. Any row order is fine.",
  "output_contract": {
    "columns": [
      {
        "name": "promo_id",
        "type_class": "numeric"
      },
      {
        "name": "promo_units_per_day",
        "type_class": "numeric"
      },
      {
        "name": "baseline_units_per_day",
        "type_class": "numeric"
      },
      {
        "name": "uplift_per_day",
